# Golden v2-compat OUTPUTS carry a byte-exact contract: the writer emits the line
# ending as a format decision, not a platform one (literal "\r\n" for v2-compat,
# never Environment.NewLine), and the golden harness compares byte-for-byte. So
# the expected .cxt/.dat must be CRLF on every OS, including Linux CI. Freeze them
# — no normalization — but they still render as textual diffs in review (no NUL
# bytes). See docs/principles.md P-9 and the determinism contract (D-004).
fixtures/v2/**/expected/*.cxt -text
fixtures/v2/**/expected/*.dat -text

# Fixture INPUTS carry no byte contract — the readers are EOL-agnostic (BedReader
# normalizes CRLF->LF before parsing; the Sep CSV reader tokenizes either) — so
# treat them as ordinary text: LF in the repo, native on checkout. This is where
# "the EOL shouldn't matter as long as the code handles both" actually applies.
fixtures/v2/**/*.bed  text
fixtures/v2/**/*.data text
